UPCOMING EVENTS (1 MINUTE PER PERSON)  {\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss\fprq2\fcharset0 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa160\sl252\slmult1\i\f0\fs22 Recreation Supervisor David Ibarra invited the community to attend the 35th Annual Manhattan Beach Pumpkin Race at the Manhattan Beach Pier on October 19, 2025, beginning at 12:00 PM, with races starting at 2:00 PM; and the 37th Annual Family Halloween Carnival at Manhattan Heights Park on October 25, 2025, from 1:00 PM to 5:00 PM.\cf1\b\i0\line\line\cf0\b0\i Library Manager Cassandra Sargent invited the community to attend a Vietnamese Street Food cooking demonstration on October 18, 2025, from 2:00 PM to 3:00 PM; and the Manhattan Beach Speaker Series featuring Nic Stone on October 25, 2025, from 2:00 PM to 4:00 PM. She also thanked Mayor Pro Tem Franklin for attending the \ldblquote Why Dolphins Surf?\rdblquote  program held last month.\line\line Congressman Ted Lieu\rquote s Office Representative Mia Thiagarajan provided an update on the government shutdown and noted that their office remains open during regular business hours to assist with constituent casework. She encouraged the public to reach out for support as needed.\line\line Environmental Programs Administrator Britny Coker-Moen announced that the City of Manhattan Beach has been recertified as a Blue City by the Blue City Network, a program of the non-profit organization Project O; mentioned that the City was the first certified Blue City in 2020; and stated that the program recognizes cities and counties for their leadership in sustainability and dedication to protecting the marine environment and waterways.\line\line Waste Management Recycling Education Representative Bobby Bell thanked the Public Works Department and staff involved with the Manhattan Beach Hometown Fair for organizing a successful event with strong community turnout; announced the final Composting Class at the Manhattan Beach Botanical Garden at Polliwog Park on October 11, 2025, at 10:00 AM; and the final Free Paper Shredding event at Mira Costa High School on October 18, 2025.\line\line Fire Engineer/Paramedic James Stratton invited the community to the 3rd Annual Manhattan Beach Fire Association Pancake Breakfast at Fire Station 1 on October 12, 2025, from 8:00 AM to 12:00 PM; and announced the celebration of the second-grade winners of the Exit Drill in the Home Program, an important school district initiative where students learn and demonstrate fire safety skills to help keep their families safe.\line\line Mayor Lesser provided brief remarks to Manhattan Beach residents regarding the Chevron Refinery Fire in El Segundo that occurred the previous week, acknowledging the fear and uncertainty experienced by the community; reassuring residents that Manhattan Beach public safety teams responded within two minutes of the incident; and expressing gratitude to the first responders, including Manhattan Beach firefighters who provided mutual aid. He noted that the cause of the fire remains under investigation and emphasized the City\rquote s commitment to working with Chevron and the City of El Segundo to improve regional emergency communication; and affirmed the City\rquote s dedication to transparency, safety, and environmental responsibility.\line\line Fire Chief Jesse Alexander addressed community concerns regarding the recent Chevron Refinery Fire in El Segundo, acknowledging the fear experienced by residents and reaffirming that public safety remains the City\rquote s top priority; commended the swift and professional response of Manhattan Beach Fire Department, which was en route within two minutes and actively engaged in suppression efforts while maintaining citywide coverage; highlighted the coordinated efforts of the Manhattan Beach Police Department in securing the area and preparing for potential evacuations; noted initial challenges in receiving real-time updates from incident command due to the complexity of the event, but emphasized that the City issued a public notification within 26 minutes of the initial call; and stated that the City is working with regional partners to review the incident and improve emergency communication protocols. He encouraged residents to sign up for Alert SouthBay to receive timely emergency notifications.\cf2\i0\f1\par
}


